How can I implement a transformation matrix in Go to rotate a set of points clockwise by an angle of 3pi/4 and update their coordinates accordingly?
Assuming the points are represented as an array of [x, y] coordinates, one way to accomplish this is to use the following transformation matrix:
[cos(3pi/4) -sin(3pi/4)]
[sin(3pi/4)  cos(3pi/4)]
You can then apply this matrix to each point using matrix multiplication to obtain their new coordinates. Here is some sample Go code to demonstrate:
```go
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "math"
)
func main() {
    points := [][2]float64{{1, 0}, {0, 1}, {-1, 0}, {0, -1}}
    // Define the transformation matrix
    theta := 3 * math.Pi / 4
    matrix := [2][2]float64{
        {math.Cos(theta), -math.Sin(theta)},
        {math.Sin(theta), math.Cos(theta)},
    }
    // Apply the transformation to each point
    transformedPoints := [][2]float64{}
    for _, p := range points {
        transformedPoint := [2]float64{}
        for i, r := range matrix {
            transformedPoint[i] = r[0]*p[0] + r[1]*p[1]
        }
        transformedPoints = append(transformedPoints, transformedPoint)
    }
    // Print the original and transformed coordinates
    fmt.Println("Original points:", points)
    fmt.Println("Transformed points:", transformedPoints)
}
```
