The formula to calculate the minimum score required to pass a test with 15 questions and a passing score of 80% is:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{Number of Correct Answers}}{\text{Total Number of Questions}} \times 100\% \geq 80\%
\end{equation}
In other words, the percentage of correct answers should be greater than or equal to 80%. To find the minimum score required, we can rearrange the equation as follows:
\begin{equation}
\frac{\text{Number of Correct Answers}}{\text{Total Number of Questions}} \geq \frac{80\%}{100\%} = 0.8
\end{equation}
Then, we can solve for the minimum number of correct answers required:
\begin{equation}
\text{Number of Correct Answers} \geq 0.8 \times \text{Total Number of Questions} = 0.8 \times 15 = 12
\end{equation}
Therefore, the minimum number of correct answers required to pass the test with a passing score of 80% is 12.

To break down a sentence into its syntactic components in Java, you can utilize a natural language processing (NLP) library like Stanford CoreNLP or Apache OpenNLP. Both libraries provide tools for tokenizing, tagging, parsing, and extracting syntactic components from text.
Here's an example code snippet using Stanford CoreNLP to extract the syntactic components of your example sentence:
```
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Properties;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.CoreLabel;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.Annotation;
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.StanfordCoreNLP;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraph;
import edu.stanford.nlp.semgraph.SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.Tree;
import edu.stanford.nlp.trees.TreeCoreAnnotations;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.CoreMap;
public class SyntacticParser {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Set up CoreNLP pipeline
    Properties props = new Properties();
    props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, lemma, parse");
    StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
    // Example sentence
    String text = "John saw the big dog bark.";
    // Annotate sentence
    Annotation document = new Annotation(text);
    pipeline.annotate(document);
    // Extract syntactic components
    List<CoreMap> sentences = document.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class);
    for (CoreMap sentence : sentences) {
      Tree tree = sentence.get(TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation.class);
      SemanticGraph dependencies = sentence.get(SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations.CollapsedCCProcessedDependenciesAnnotation.class);
      System.out.println("Tree: " + tree);
      System.out.println("Dependencies: " + dependencies);
    }
  }
}
```
This code uses the Stanford CoreNLP library to tokenize, split, tag, and parse the sentence. The resulting `Annotation` object contains a list of `CoreMap` objects, each representing a sentence in the input text. We can then extract the parse tree and dependency graph for each sentence using the `TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation` and `SemanticGraphCoreAnnotations.CollapsedCCProcessedDependenciesAnnotation` annotations, respectively.
Once you have the parse tree and dependency graph, you can traverse them to extract the syntactic components you're interested in. For example, you can use the `Tree` class to extract noun phrases, verb phrases, adverb phrases, and prepositional phrases by navigating the tree structure and looking for specific node labels. Similarly, you can use the `SemanticGraph` class to extract clauses and other syntactic elements by looking for specific dependency relations.
Here's an example of how you might extract the noun phrases, verb phrases, and prepositional phrases from the parse tree:
```
// Extract noun phrases, verb phrases, and prepositional phrases
List<Tree> npList = tree.getLeaves().stream()
    .filter(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree).label().value().startsWith("NP"))
    .map(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
List<Tree> vpList = tree.getLeaves().stream()
    .filter(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree).label().value().startsWith("VP"))
    .map(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
List<Tree> ppList = tree.getLeaves().stream()
    .filter(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree).label().value().startsWith("PP"))
    .map(leaf -> leaf.parent(tree))
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
System.out.println("Noun phrases: " + npList);
System.out.println("Verb phrases: " + vpList);
System.out.println("Prepositional phrases: " + ppList);
```
This code uses the `Tree` class to extract all the leaf nodes that are children of a node labeled "NP", "VP", or "PP". It then collects the parent nodes of these leaves to obtain the complete noun phrases, verb phrases, and prepositional phrases in the sentence.
Note that this is just a simple example, and there are many other ways to extract syntactic components from a sentence using NLP libraries like Stanford CoreNLP or Apache OpenNLP. The exact approach you choose will depend on your specific requirements and the structure of the input text.

You can use the following R code to categorize your list of items into departments based on keyword matching:
```
# Create a data frame with your list of items
items <- data.frame(item = c("Soccer Ball", "Dress Shirt", "Smartphone", "Blender"))
# Create a data frame with the department keywords
departments <- data.frame(department = c("Sports", "Apparel", "Electronics", "Kitchen"),
                          keyword = c("soccer|basketball|tennis", "shirt|dress|pants", "smartphone|laptop|tablet", "blender|toaster|coffee maker"))
# Use a for loop to match each item to its corresponding department
for (i in 1:nrow(items)) {
  for (j in 1:nrow(departments)) {
    if (grepl(departments$keyword[j], items$item[i], ignore.case = TRUE)) {
      items$department[i] <- departments$department[j]
      break
    }
  }
}
# View the resulting data frame with the items and their corresponding departments
items
```
This code creates two data frames - one with the list of items and one with the department keywords. It then uses a for loop to match each item to its corresponding department based on keyword matching using the `grepl` function. Finally, it adds a new column to the items data frame with the department for each item and displays the resulting data frame.

